1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,098
Every lecture up to this point has been about making a batch worth testing: briefed

2
00:00:05,098 --> 00:00:08,930
properly, generated at volume, checked for every way it could fail.

3
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:12,648
None of that answers the actual question this course opened with.

4
00:00:12,648 --> 00:00:13,506
Which one wins.

5
00:00:13,506 --> 00:00:17,967
This section builds the thing that actually answers it: budgets and kill rules

6
00:00:17,967 --> 00:00:22,658
decided before the data arrives, a naming system that survives six months, and the

7
00:00:22,658 --> 00:00:26,890
statistical honesty to read a small-spend result without fooling yourself.

8
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:30,723
Harlan's own testing architecture is the worked example throughout.

9
00:00:31,273 --> 00:00:35,589
Four lectures, then a workshop where you build your own testing architecture from

10
00:00:35,589 --> 00:00:36,016
scratch.

11
00:00:36,016 --> 00:00:40,226
By the end of this section, you'll have a structure ready for a real test, with

12
00:00:40,226 --> 00:00:44,649
every rule that governs it decided in advance, not improvised while the numbers are

13
00:00:44,649 --> 00:00:45,609
already coming in.
